    {
      "name": "a fresh mark honours any number at or above one",
      "law": "The seq and the leash",
      "obligations": ["VIII-1", "VIII-2", "VII-4", "III-16"],
      "derivation": "Article VIII: \"Which number a caller opens with, above one, is the caller's own. A fresh mark is empty, so every number at or above one stands above it: the door honours what arrives and the mark moves there. No door requires a first message to carry exactly one.\" So seq 7 is honoured against an empty mark and the mark becomes 7, with nothing beneath it spent. The way back is learned here, the seq having been spent. **And this door has already refused a message and is still standing, which is Article III's rule for the line read at distance zero**: \"**A well-formed frame whose envelope fails the judgment is ordinary silence, and the line lives on.**\" The exchange before this one was refused at step 5 and answered with nothing. A door that treated a failed judgment as a fatal condition — closing, resetting its records, or refusing everything after — would meet this ask with silence too, and the whole of the rest of this scenario with it. **Eight more exchanges follow, five of them after further refusals**, so the claim is asserted nine times over rather than once, and no single exchange in the suite could have carried it.",
